Ista,

Thanks for your suggestion.  I think we were headed in the same direction.  What I came up with is something like this.

testdata <- data.frame(
    a = c(' ', '1', '2', '3', ' ', '14', '15'),
    b = c(' ', 'a', '', '', '',  'b', ''),
    c = c(' ', 'A', 'B', 'C', ' ', 'N', 'O'),
    d = c(' ', 'X', 'Y', 'Z', ' ', 'y', 'z'),
    stringsAsFactors = FALSE)

desired <- data.frame(

    P1 = c('1', '1', '14'),
    P2 = c('a', 'a', 'b'),
    P3 = c('A', 'A', 'N'),
    P4 = c('X', 'X', 'y'),
    C1 = c('2', '3', '15'),
    C2 = c('B', 'C', 'O'),
    C3 = c('Y', 'Z', 'z'),
    stringsAsFactors = FALSE)

empty_as_na <- function(x){

    if("factor" %in% class(x)) x <- as.character(x) ## since ifelse wont work with factors
    ifelse(as.character(x)!="", x, NA)
}

testdata <- testdata %>% mutate_each(funs(empty_as_na))

nrows <- dim(testdata)[1]
recordcount <- 0          ## Counter for 1401-inspired record mark.
testdata$recordnum <- NA  ## Field to store record mark.
recordmark  <- 2          ## Column with field that only appears in parent entries.

for (i in 1:nrows) {
    ## Increment recordcount on parent record.
    if(!is.na(testdata[i, recordmark])) recordcount <- recordcount + 1;
    ## Write recordcount into recordnum field.
    testdata[i, 'recordnum'] <- recordcount
}

parents <- testdata %>%
    group_by(recordnum) %>%
    slice(1)
colnames(parents) <- c(c('a','b','c','d'),'recordnum')
parents <- parents

children <- testdata %>%
    group_by(recordnum) %>%
    ## Delete parent row and blank separator row.
    filter((row_number() !=1) & (row_number() != n()))
colnames(children) <- c(c('a','b','c','d'),'recordnum')
children <- children %>%
    dplyr::select(-starts_with('X'))

results <- parents %>%
    right_join(children, by = 'recordnum')

That's not quite right; my real versionhas a row of parent headers and then a row of child headers, and I carried those along and put them in the right places.  This code also has a small defect I don't have time to find right now; it seems to miss the second record, and the numbers are incremented by 1, neither of which happen in my production code.

Thanks for your contribution.

Bill

